A **frequency map** counts how many times each character appears in a string. In JavaScript, use a `Map` or a plain object for this. For lowercase English letters, an array of 26 counters (`new Array(26).fill(0)`) indexed by `char.charCodeAt(0) - 97` is the fastest option.

## Anagram Detection

Two strings are anagrams if and only if their frequency maps are identical. Implementation: build the frequency map for `s1`, then for each character in `s2`, decrement its count. If all counts are zero at the end, they're anagrams.

```javascript
function isAnagram(s, t) {
  if (s.length !== t.length) return false;
  const freq = new Array(26).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
    freq[s.charCodeAt(i) - 97]++;
    freq[t.charCodeAt(i) - 97]--;
  }
  return freq.every(x => x === 0);
}
```

## Grouping Anagrams

To group strings by anagram equivalence, sort each string's characters to get a canonical key. Strings with the same sorted key are anagrams of each other. Use the sorted string as a hash map key.

**Two pointers** on strings work identically to two pointers on arrays - strings are indexable.

## Palindrome Check

Place one pointer at the start (`lo = 0`) and one at the end (`hi = s.length - 1`). If `s[lo] === s[hi]`, both pointers move inward. If they ever differ, it's not a palindrome. If the pointers meet without mismatch, it is.

```javascript
function isPalindrome(s) {
  let lo = 0, hi = s.length - 1;
  while (lo < hi) {
    if (s[lo] !== s[hi]) return false;
    lo++; hi--;
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Reversing a String

In-place reversal: convert to array (strings are immutable), swap `arr[lo]` with `arr[hi]` using two pointers until they meet, then join.

`str.split('').reverse().join('')` is the concise version - correct for ASCII, but fails for multi-byte Unicode characters (emoji, certain CJK characters) where `split('')` splits surrogate pairs.

**The cost of `str += char` in a loop** is O(n²) total due to JavaScript string immutability. Each concatenation allocates a new string of length 1, 2, 3, ..., n - summing to O(n²).

**The fix**: use an array as a buffer, push characters into it, and call `.join('')` once at the end.

```javascript
// SLOW: O(n²) total allocations
let result = '';
for (const char of chars) result += char;

// FAST: O(n) total
const buf = [];
for (const char of chars) buf.push(char);
const result = buf.join('');
```

For most interview problems with n ≤ 10⁴, the difference is unnoticeable. But for n ≥ 10⁵ string building problems, this distinction can be the difference between passing and TLEing.

